  "description": "A quick, practical guide to Thermoception: How TRP Channels Detect Temperature.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "You yank your hand off a hot stove before you even feel pain. Temperature sensing is so critical it has its own fast-track wiring.",
      "visual": "🔥"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "TRP channels are molecular thermometers embedded in nerve endings. Each type opens at a specific temperature range.",
      "visual": "🌡️"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "TRPV1 activates above 43 degrees Celsius, signaling dangerous heat. TRPM8 activates below 26 degrees, signaling cold.",
      "visual": "🧬"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Capsaicin fools TRPV1 into opening at body temperature, which is why chili peppers feel hot even though nothing is burning.",
      "visual": "🌶️"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "The hypothalamus integrates thermal data from the skin and the body core to regulate sweating, shivering, and blood flow.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Your survival depends on temperature awareness.",
      "visual": "🛡️"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What role do TRP channels play in thermoception?",
    "options": [
      "They are molecular sensors that open at specific temperature ranges",
      "They regulate blood pressure in the heart",
      "They transmit visual signals to the cortex"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
